.TH heliwm 1 "Version 1.13"
.SH NAME
Heliwm \- Highly Essential Light Itsy-bitsy Window Manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
heliwm [ \-d \fIdisplay\fP ] [ \-f \fIfilename\fP ] [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Heliwm
is one of the smallest window manager for X Window System.  Its design goal is
minimum consumption of memory and CPU time for saving natural resources, so it
provides nothing more than basic window operations.  They include window moving,
resizing, iconifying, raising, lowering and change of the keyboard focus.
Window maximizing operation, frame drawing and a few other things are optional.
No menus, gorgeous look, virtual screens, etc.

All window manipulations can be done with a keyboard.  The idea, that they can
be done without using a pointing device, is inherited from Robert
Nation's \fIfvwm\fP(1).  Thank you, Rob.

Heliwm
was named after the second smallest element, helium.  I wanted to name it after
the smallest one, but the name hydrogen doesn't end with "-um," as you may see,
so I selected the name.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP 4
\-d \fIdisplay\fP
Specify X server to use.

.TP 4
\-f \fIfilename\fP
Specify non-default configuration file to use.  This option doesn't work as
expected without RCPARSER compile time option (see below).

.TP 4
\-v
Print version number along with compile time options and exit.

.SH COMPILE TIME OPTIONS
There are several compile time options, each of which increases the binary size.

.IP FRAME
If heliwm is compiled without this option, it is called
.I alpha particle
version.  It doesn't decorate client windows with frames, in order to save even more
resources.

.IP RCPARSER
Configuration file parsing capability.  Without this option, heliwm can only
use its hard-coded configuration information.

.IP NLS
Native language system support.
In order to use character sets other than ISO-8859-1 for window and icon
titles, set environmental variable
.I LANG
to the name of your locale (e.g.,
.I ja_JP
for Japanese).  Ensure that
.I f
option in your configuration file has a comma-separated list of required fonts.
If you don't know which fonts are required, run heliwm with
.I fixed
specified for the
.I f
option and it will report missing character sets, if any.  Use
\fIxlsfonts\fP(1) to look for fonts for each character set.

.IP SHAPE
X non-rectangular window shape extension support.  Clients such as
\fIoclock\fP(1) and \fIxeyes\fP(1) are written using the extension, but can be
run without it.  Some non-rectangular windows generate many X events upon
exposure, and can be a burden to CPU, so avoid using them as much as possible.
Alpha particle version doesn't need this option, since it displays
non-rectangular windows as they are.

.IP MAXIMIZE
Optional window maximizing feature.

.IP REORDER
Optional window list reordering feature.  Heliwm manages windows in its
internal list, and by using this feature, it is possible to reorder this list.
For details, see "reorder" in OPERATIONS section below.

.SH CONFIGURATION
When heliwm is compiled with RCPARSER option, a configuration file is read upon
startup.  Three configuration file paths are checked in following order: one
specified with
.I \-f
option, \fI~/.heliwmrc\fP,
system default configuration file.  The one which heliwm finds first is used.
If none of the three is
available, or heliwm is compiled without RCPARSER, built-in values are used.

Configuration file format is quite strict, and all invalid lines are silently
ignored.  First of all, a line must start with an option key, which is an
alphabet letter.  Some of them require a sub-key, which must be the second
character on the line.  Keys and sub-keys are case-sensitive.  Each word is
separated by exactly one character, which may be a space or a tab.  Take a look
at the default configuration file for an example.

If there is a syntax error in the file, heliwm print out an error message and exit.
Look for it if heliwm doesn't seem running.

Because heliwm treats a line starting with a hash mark ("#") as an "invalid"
line and silently ignores it, comments can be added using a hash as the first
character on a line.

Here is a list of available customization options:
.IP "c\fIS color_name\fP"
Specify color to use.
.I S
here is one of following sub-keys: t, f, b, u, l, a.  They correspond to colors
of text, frame, border (of icons and indicator window), upper-left shade of
frame, lower-right shade of frame, and band, respectively.

Text color is used for window title text, and band color is used for a
move/resize band.  You may need to choose a color for the latter, so that it is
in a good contrast with most part of your screen, or you may find the band hard
to see.

Border color is used to draw border lines around icons and the indicator
window.

Frame, upper-left and lower-right shade colors are used to draw frame around
client windows.  If you want a pseudo 3D look, specify a color darker than
frame for lower-right shade, and a color brighter than frame for upper-left
shade, for example.  Alpha particle version ignores these colors, since it
doesn't draw frames.

Use \fIshowrgb\fP(1) to find what color names are available.
.IP "w\fIS\fP \fIwidth\fP"
Specify frame width in integer.
.I S
is either
.I f
for frame, or
.I b
for border of icons and the indicator window.  Alpha particle version ignores
the former.
.IP "f \fIfont_name\fP"
Specify font for window and icon titles.  They are written in
"text" color, specified with
.I ct
option.
.IP "C \fIcursor_number\fP"
Specify cursor to use.  Cursor number can be obtained from
<X11/cursorfont.h>.  To preview cursor shapes, use \fIxfd\fP(1) with
\fI-fn cursor\fP option.
.IP "k\fIS modifier key\fP"
Specify window manipulation key.
.I S
here is one of operation sub-keys: \fIm\fP, \fIr\fP, \fIi\fP, \fIn\fP, \fIp\fP,
\fIa\fP, \fIl\fP, \fId\fP, \fIt\fP, \fIx\fP, \fIh\fP, \fIv\fP, \fIo\fP.  See
section OPERATIONS for details.
.I Modifier
is the sum of one or more integers representing modifier keys.  See section
MODIFIERS for details.
.I Key
is one of the key names in <X11/keysymdef.h>, with the prefix XK_ removed.
.IP "b\fIS modifier button\fP"
Specify window manipulation button.
.I S
and
.I modifier
are the same as those for key option.
.I Button
is mouse button number: 1 through 5.
.IP "o \fIother_option\fP"
Specify other options.  There are eight of them, and a number is assigned to
each.  Pick up options you want and add their numbers, or specify 0 if you want
none of them.
.RS
.IP 1 4
Set keyboard focus to a window when it is raised.
.IP 2 4
Set keyboard focus to a window when the pointer is circulated to it.
.IP 4 4
Set keyboard focus to a window when it is mapped (displayed).
.IP 8 4
Raise a window when the pointer is circulated to it.
.IP 16 4
Move the pointer to upper-left corner of a window, when it is focused.
.IP 32 4
Set original window border width, which many programs set with "-bw" option, to
zero automatically, upon mapping the window.
.IP 64 4
Redraw frame of active window only.  Active window is a window with the
keyboard focus, if any, or one with the circulation pointer.  When there is no
active window, all window frames are redrawn.  Specifying this option
significantly saves CPU load.  This option is meaningless for alpha particle
version.
.IP 128 4
When a window is to be mapped without user-specified location, place it
interactively by moving a band for it.  If this option is not specified, the
window will be placed at where the application specifies.
.PP
"Circulated" here means when "next" (\fIn\fP sub-key) or "previous" (\fIp\fP
sub-key) window operations are executed.

.SH OPERATIONS

Following operation sub-keys are available, with default key binding in
parenthesis:

.IP \fIm\fP 3
Trigger move operation (Alt-F8).

.IP \fIr\fP 3
Trigger resize operation (Alt-F7).

.IP \fIi\fP 3
Iconify/deiconify a window (Alt-F9).

.IP \fIn\fP 3
Move the pointer to next window (Alt-Tab).

.IP \fIp\fP 3
Move the pointer to previous window (Alt-Shift-Tab).

.IP \fIa\fP 3
Raise a window (Alt-F2).

.IP \fIl\fP 3
Lower a window (Alt-F3).

.IP \fId\fP 3
Delete a window, if it defines WM_DELETE_WINDOW protocol (Alt-F4).  If it
doesn't, it cannot be deleted by heliwm.

.IP \fIt\fP 3
Terminate heliwm (Control-Shift-Escape).

.IP \fIx\fP 3
Maximize/unmaximize a window (Alt-F10).  This is available only when heliwm is
compiled with MAXIMIZE compile time option.

.IP \fIh\fP 3
Maximize/unmaximize a window horizontally (Alt-F11).  This is available only when heliwm is
compiled with MAXIMIZE compile time option.

.IP \fIx\fP 3
Maximize/unmaximize a window vertically (Alt-F12).  This is available only when heliwm is
compiled with MAXIMIZE compile time option.

.IP \fIo\fP 3
Reorder internal window management list by bringing a window to the top of the
list. (Alt-F1).  This is available only when heliwm is compiled with REORDER
compile time option.

.PP
Move and resize operations are triggered by pressing a key or a mouse button
with zero or more optional modifier keys, which are described below.  The
cursor changes, a band appears to show current position and size of the window,
and an indicator window appears at the center of the screen.  Use arrow keys
(up, down, left and right), \fIvi\fP(1) style (k, j, h and l) or \fIemacs\fP(1)
style (p, n, b and f) cursor movement keys to move or resize the window.
Holding down a shift key makes the change smaller, while a control key makes it
even smaller.  Modifier 1 key, if available, makes it larger.  Press Enter key
or a button to complete the operation.

When a window is iconified for the first time, and interactive placement option
(128 of "other_options"; see above) is specified, the band shows up for
placement of the icon.  Use the keys to move it to anywhere you want.

.I n
operation moves the pointer to upper-left corner of next window in heliwm's
internal window management list.
.I p
operation moves the pointer to previous one.

By using optional reorder operation, it is possible to bring a window to the
top of the list.

Maximizing a window makes it as large as possible within the size of the
screen.  When a client has a maximum size set, heliwm admires it, so it is
impossible to make the window larger than the size.  Similarly, when the client
has a minimum size set, it is impossible to make the window smaller than that.

.SH MODIFIERS

Following modifiers are available:
.IP 0 3
Require no modifier to be pressed.
.IP 1 3
Require a shift key to be pressed.
.IP 2 3
Require a control key to be pressed.
.IP 4 3
Require a modifier 1 key to be pressed.
.IP 8 3
Require a modifier 2 key to be pressed.
.PP
For example, if you want a modifier 1 key and a shift key to be pressed while
pressing a key, specify 5 as \fImodifier\fP.  Run \fIxmodmap\fP(1) to see what
keys are assigned to modifier 1 and 2.

.SH MEMORY AND CPU TIME SAVING TIPS

Compile heliwm without FRAME option to make it alpha particle version.

Avoid wrapping windows as much as possible.  When an obscured part is exposed,
it has to be redrawn, consuming extra CPU time.  Users of heliwm compiled with
FRAME option are encouraged to use "redraw frame of active window only" option.
See the description of option
.I o
in section CONFIGURATION.

Use \fIrxvt\fP(1) instead of \fIxterm\fP(1) when possible.

Use text console instead of \fIX\fP(1) or other window systems whenever
possible.  I developed heliwm mostly on a text console. :-)

.SH FILES

.TP
/usr/X11R6/lib/X11/heliwm/heliwmrc
System default run control file.
.TP
~/.heliwmrc
User run control file.

